Rejected; no family is shipped.

The paper’s worst-case NP-completeness theorem does not establish hardness for the generated distribution, and the honest Track B prototype was solved at every permitted oracle rung. Final verdict: `too_easy` after three escalations, including `n=420`.

- Full rationale: [REJECTED.md](/Users/jang-wongi/Desktop/WG/PI_LAB/arxiv-gv-problems/results/2007.05020/REJECTED.md)
- Reproducible draft: [gen_2007_05020.py](/Users/jang-wongi/Desktop/WG/PI_LAB/arxiv-gv-problems/results/2007.05020/gen_2007_05020.py)
- Official evidence: [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/Users/jang-wongi/Desktop/WG/PI_LAB/arxiv-gv-problems/results/2007.05020/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- Paper reviewed: [Dota Underlords game is NP-complete](https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.05020)

The draft passed its local G1–G8 checks, including 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses and five attacks at 0/8. However, Grok returned a verified witness on the terminal escalated rung. Per Step 4, I did not retune further or create shipping reports, README, or G9 transcripts.
